    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §350-c-1, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7558\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the general business law, in relation to disclosures\nrequired for certain solicitations by container\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nThis bill requires containers used for solicitation of property such\nto have labels which identify the owner and its contact information\nand disclose whether the property collected will be used for\ncharitable purposes or for profit.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one adds a new section 350-c-1 to the general municipal law to\nrequire owners of containers for the collection of property to\ndisclose on such container the name, address and telephone number of\nthe owner or its agent and state that the property collected will be\nused for charitable purposes and its value is tax deductible, or the\nproperty collected will be used for profit and its value is not tax\ndeductible. Recyclables are excluded from this provision. Any\nviolation will be deemed a deceptive practice within the meaning of\n§349 of the general municipal law and its remedies are available for\nenforcement.\n\nSection 2 sets the effective day as 180 days after this bill becomes\nlaw.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nNearly everyone who donates clothing at a collection bin expects that\nthe donated items will go to help someone in need. Many of these\ndonation bins, however, are collection points for commercial\nindustries which sell donated clothing, for example, at market prices\nfor their own profit. The \"rag trade\" is a billion dollar industry.\nWhile this textile recycling is not illegal, its use of not-for-profit\norganizations and their charity collection bins to collect materials\nused or sold for profit deceives the public.\n\nThis bill does what a number of states have done - it requires notice\nto the public. Owners of containers which collect property such as\nclothing must tell the public if the property collected will be used\nfor charitable purposes or for commercial profit. This legislation\nwould thereby prohibit commercial businesses from mimicking\nnot-for-profit clothing drop-offs to collect, resell and make a profit\nfrom recycled rags, insulation and other donated goods.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill shall be effective 180 days after it becomes law.\n",
