    "lawCode": "Add §399-bbb, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S7558A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the general business law, in relation to disclosures required\nfor certain solicitations by container\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\nThis bill requires containers used\nfor solicitation of certain personal property to have labels which\nidentify the owner and its contact information and disclose whether\nthe property collected will be used for charitable purposes or for\nprofit.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\nSection one adds a new section 399-bbb\nto the general municipal law to require owners of containers for the\ncollection of property to disclose on such container the name,\naddress and telephone number of the owner or its agent and state that\nthe property collected will be used for charitable purposes and its\nvalue is tax deductible or the property collected will be used for\nprofit and its value is not tax deductible. In addition, the owner of\nthe container must obtain written consent from the owner of the\nproperty where the container will be placed. Recyclables are excluded\nfrom this provision. Any violation will be deemed a deceptive\npractice within the meaning of §349 of the general municipal law and\nits remedies are available for enforcement. As such, \"collection\ncontainer\" is defined in order to clarify any questions regarding\nwhat containers will be affected by enactment of this legislation.\nSection 2 sets the effective day as 180 days after this bill becomes\nlaw and shall apply to all existing and future collection containers.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nNearly everyone who donates clothing at a collection\nbin expects that the donated items will go to help someone in need.\nMany of these donation bins, however, are collection points for\ncommercial industries which sell donated clothing, for example, at\nmarket prices for their own profit. The \"rag trade\" is a billion\ndollar industry. While this textile recycling is not illegal, its use\nof not-for-profit organizations and their charity collection bins to\ncollect materials used or sold for profit deceives the public.\n\nThis bill does what a number of states have done; it requires notice\nto the public. Owners of containers which collect property such as\nclothing must tell the public if the property collected will be used\nfor charitable purposes or for commercial profit This legislation\nwould thereby prohibit commercial businesses from mimicking\nnot-for-profit clothing drop-offs to collect, re-sell and make a\nprofit from recycled rags, insulation and other donated goods.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNew bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone.\n\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill shall be effective 180 days after it becomes\nlaw and shall apply to all existing and future collection containers.\n\n",
